WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.180
Speaker 1: All right. I want to start today&nbsp;
with yet another reminder based on a new study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.180
that gun violence is dramatically worse in red&nbsp;
states. One of the big narratives that is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:19.200
to play a significant role in the 2024 election&nbsp;
is that of violent crime and murder and guns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:26.280
Republicans pointing to so-called Democrat cities&nbsp;
and suggesting that urban progressives, I guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.820
are responsible for violence and gun violence&nbsp;
and crime. This is very much not true. There's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.220
really good piece in Politico magazine called Gun&nbsp;
Violence is actually worse in red states. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:45.420
even close. And it looks at this issue in a number&nbsp;
of different ways. And one of the findings of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:52.020
study is that gun deaths are far less common in&nbsp;
the New York City area than in the U.S. Overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.360
debunking one of the very often repeated&nbsp;
claims from the right that New York City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:03.180
is some kind of dangerous hellhole. And the&nbsp;
article explains that if you grew up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:10.680
coal mining region of eastern Pennsylvania, your&nbsp;
chance of dying of a gunshot is about half. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:18.660
you grew up in the coal fields of West Virginia,&nbsp;
similar regions, relatively rural and relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:24.900
conservative. But you have stricter gun laws&nbsp;
in blue Pennsylvania than in red West Virginia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:31.080
and you're much less likely to die of a gunshot.&nbsp;
And as you read the article, whether you divide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:39.900
the country up regionally or state to state, you&nbsp;
find that historically and presently blue areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:46.740
are significantly safer when it comes to gun&nbsp;
violence. It's beyond the scope of this segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:52.500
to go through the entire piece with you. But&nbsp;
it divides the country up into all sorts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.660
different areas and looks at this in so many&nbsp;
different ways. The Deep South has some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.400
highest rates of gun death among major regions,&nbsp;
and it's one of the most conservative parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.940
the country. Great Appalachia has the highest&nbsp;
rate of gun suicides among major regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:15.240
and that is a right wing area. And you go on&nbsp;
and on and you find that this is, quite frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.080
an undeniable reality. Now, one of the important&nbsp;
things to remember about why this isn't going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.760
convince the deniers and will link to this in the&nbsp;
description to the YouTube video, and I encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:32.820
you to look at it and check it out. And, you know,&nbsp;
maybe it will change some minds. But one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:38.880
problems with this issue and I've explained this&nbsp;
before, is that, number one, violence and crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:46.140
are genuinely really complicated issues that can&nbsp;
relate to culture, but also economics, gun laws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:53.160
access to mental health services, so many&nbsp;
different things. This doesn't mean we ignore any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:59.100
one aspect or focus on only one aspect. It just&nbsp;
means it's complicated. But the trend line is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:04.620
gun violence is much worse in red states. One of&nbsp;
the things that will often happen is if you say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.600
Oh, the red states are where the problem is,&nbsp;
you'll have some right wingers come in and say no.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.760
But if you look at the red states where the real&nbsp;
problem is, is in the blue cities and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:21.180
by sheer numbers, it can sometimes be the case&nbsp;
that it is worse in cities. But when you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.140
at rates that account for population density,&nbsp;
it stops being the case. But then if you say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:31.620
well, it's a blue city, but all the violence&nbsp;
is in the areas represented by the conservative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.660
city councilors or whatever the case may&nbsp;
be, you can zoom in or out strategically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.600
in order to get the outcome that you want,&nbsp;
and that can also make this a difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:50.940
conversation to have. But the big story, which is&nbsp;
conservatives blaming blue Democrats, cities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:58.380
urban gun violence on gangs and welfare queens,&nbsp;
and pretending that this isn't an issue in rural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:04.380
and conservative West Virginia or the right wing&nbsp;
Deep South or whatever the case may be is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:11.160
not true. One other thing. The right loves to talk&nbsp;
about, you know, Chicago and Baltimore as these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:17.700
crime ridden hellholes. The truth is that even&nbsp;
within cities that have gun violence problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:26.640
the gun violence is overwhelmingly concentrated&nbsp;
in a few areas. Most of Chicago, most of New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.340
York City, most of Baltimore does not actually&nbsp;
have these issues. This is aside from whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:37.620
the issues relate to a particular politics or&nbsp;
how it relates to the state or whatever the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.420
may be, even within these so-called dangerous&nbsp;
cities. The violence is overwhelmingly only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:48.000
in certain areas, and this makes it a really&nbsp;
difficult issue to tease out one particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:55.260
cause. What we know when it comes to solving&nbsp;
the problem is that before even, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:01.080
just on principle, so many on the American right&nbsp;
don't even. One to consider the fact that guns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.000
guns and gun availability may have something to do&nbsp;
with it. And as I've said before, but let's talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.480
about mental health. Let's talk about culture.&nbsp;
Let's talk about poverty. Let's talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.220
education. Let's talk about all those things. But&nbsp;
we can't exclude guns from the gun problem. Guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:20.640
have to be part of that discussion. And check out&nbsp;
the Politico magazine piece. It's very, very good.

